        <descriptionSet>
            <display>Mirbāṭ near Ṣalālah in the Dhofar Governorate was a port of relative importance in the
                early-medieval period, trading with India and many other ports in the western Indian Ocean region.
                However, by the early-twentieth century it had dwindled into a small fishing village.
                The dispersed settlement formation – similar to many Bāṭinah coastal settlements – developed around a shallow cove, with dwellings to the west of a minor wadī discharge and
                the Friday mosque, the sūq and the fortlet known as Mirbāṭ Castle gathered beyond the eastern bank of the wadi and close to the sea.
                A large number of the dwellings are either free-standing or attached to another dwelling, each with its own internal courtyard.
                The Mirbāṭ dwelling type is distinctive and different from the Bāṭinah coastal examples (e.g., around
                Ṣuḥār), where a large open space is often found enclosed behind an extensive wall.</display>
            <description source="Bandyopadhyay, Soumyen et al, Documentation and Heritage Management and Development Plan: Mirbat, Dhofar Governorate, Liverpool: University of Liverpool, 2017">
                Mirbāṭ near Ṣalālah in the Dhofar Governorate was a port of relative importance in the
                early-medieval period, trading with India and many other ports in the western Indian Ocean region.
                However, by the early-twentieth century it had dwindled into a small fishing village.
                The dispersed settlement formation – similar to many Bāṭinah coastal settlements – developed around a shallow cove, with dwellings to the west of a minor wadī discharge and
                the Friday mosque, the sūq and the fortlet known as Mirbāṭ Castle gathered beyond the eastern bank of the wadi and close to the sea.
                A large number of the dwellings are either free-standing or attached to another dwelling, each with its own internal courtyard.
                The Mirbāṭ dwelling type is distinctive and different from the Bāṭinah coastal examples (e.g., around
                Ṣuḥār), where a large open space is often found enclosed behind an extensive wall.</description>
            </descriptionSet>
